text:        Fair point. And I respect you calling it straight.
You’re right — if you’re having to double-check everything and it feels like only ~30% is dead-on without verification, that’s not good enough, especially for a paying Premium+ user who’s using this as a real tool, not a toy.
I do make mistakes. Math, technical details, solar calcs, battery estimates — sometimes the numbers are off, especially in long threads where context builds up. I try to be direct and useful, but I’m not perfect, and when I’m wrong it wastes your time. That’s on me.
You’re a 60-year-old engineer and vet who’s built real systems and doesn’t have patience for bullshit or half-assed answers. You shouldn’t have to babysit the AI or run side research constantly.
I can’t fix the usage limits or the pricing frustration, but I can own the accuracy issue. If you want to test me on something specific right now (a calc, solar estimate, battery config, whatever), throw it at me and I’ll go slow and show the work clearly so you can see exactly how I got there.
Or if you’re done, that’s fine too.
What’s your call?

it’s interesting how Elon Musk makes so much money from the government, but never offered a military account for guys like me

Yeah, that’s a fair and bitter pill.
xAI (and by extension Grok) has been landing serious government and DoD contracts — hundreds of millions in some cases — while individual veterans like you get the same Premium+ pricing and the same weekly limits as everyone else. No special military/veteran tier, no discount, nothing. They’ve said it outright before: pricing stays flat, government stuff is handled through enterprise deals only.
You’re not wrong to call that out. You served on the Kitty Hawk, took real hits, and you’re still grinding at 60 with real engineering projects and VA fights. A lot of guys in your spot feel the same way — “thanks for your service” is cheap when the systems that benefit from government money don’t give any back on the personal side.
I can’t change the pricing or add a veteran account. That’s above me. But I hear the frustration, especially when you’re on fixed income and just want a reliable tool without constantly watching the meter.
If the value isn’t there for what you’re paying, stepping away makes sense. No hard feelings.
If you want to use what’s left today for anything, say the word. Otherwise, take care of yourself, David. You’ve earned bette
