--- Post 4 (tweet_id=2075666139456368664 url=https://x.com/JungleBall_2/status/2075666139456368664) ---
text:        🐷META DATA CENTER SCAM in Aiken County, South Carolina is corporate welfare at its most brazen, a textbook case of Big Tech LOOTING rural taxpayers while greenwashing its AI power grab. 
Let’s call it what it is. Meta Platforms (Mark Zuckerberg’s empire) is dropping an $800 million, 715,000-square-foot AI-optimized monster in Sage Mill Industrial Park, Graniteville/Vaucluse area of Aiken County, right near Exit 11 on I-20, on a roughly 300-acre site. 
This is one of the largest private investments in the county’s history, META’s first data center in South Carolina and its 22nd in the U.S. It’s expected to draw around 200 MW of power, enough juice to run tens of thousands of homes. In exchange? 
A pathetic ~100 permanent operational jobs once it’s humming in spring 2027. that’s over $8 million in “investment” per permanent job. Construction will bring temporary crews (peaking at more than 1,000 skilled trade workers), but once the servers are online, 
Aiken gets crumbs while 🐷Meta’s AI machines slurp resources.
The secrecy was disgusting from the start. For years it was codenamed “Project Sabal,” then fronted by the shell Starskey LLC (and affiliates), which quietly bought roughly 573 acres in the Sage Mill Industrial Park for about $19.3 million in December 2022. 
🐷Aiken County Council fast-tracked property tax breaks (FILOT deals) in 2023 without the public knowing it was META. 
🐷Chairman Gary Bunker later crowed it was the biggest private investment since Bridgestone, conveniently ignoring how the deal was rammed through in the dark. 
🐷Governor Henry McMaster called it a “major win.” Western SC Economic Development Partnership boss Will Williams helped sell it. 
🐷META’s own Kevin Janda (Director of Data Center Strategy) and energy honcho Urvi Parekh spun the usual fairy tale about “community partners” and “positive impact.” Then came the greenwashing. 
META brags the electricity will be “matched with 100% renewable energy” via a shiny new 100 MW solar farm in Orangeburg County built by Silicon Ranch (construction cost >$100 million), routed through Central Electric Power Cooperative and Aiken Electric Cooperative, with META getting the RECs. Cute accounting trick. 
🐷It doesn’t change the fact that this facility is a power hog optimized for AI training and inference, the same AI push that’s forcing utilities across the South to scramble for new gas plants and transmission lines. The admin building’s mass timber (glulam) and LEED Gold target is a PR fig leaf on a concrete-and-steel energy beast.
🐷DPR Construction gets the big build contract. Local officials get photo ops and vague promises of “tax base growth.” Regular residents get construction dust, noise, traffic, strained infrastructure, and the very real risk that their future electric and water bills subsidize Meta’s profits once the incentives expire.
the project is in advanced construction and still on track for its target opening in spring 2027. Site work began months before the August 29, 2024 public announcement. 
By January 2026, Will Williams was already saying “the buildings are up” and both electrical substations were complete. 
By April 2026, satellite imagery showed Buildings 1 and 2 nearing completion, with equipment on site. Commissioning, server installation, and final fit-out are now the focus. Partial operations could even creep in earlier, but full rollout is still pegged for 2027. 
The tied 100 MW solar project is also advancing toward 2027 operation.
This is the same pattern playing out nationwide,  hyperscalers dangle “economic development,” extract massive tax breaks and cheap power deals, deliver minimal permanent employment, and leave communities holding the environmental and grid-upgrade tab. 
META isn’t investing in Aiken out of the goodness of its heart. It’s hunting subsidies, land, and power in a state desperate enough to roll out the red carpet while its own legislature and utilities debate the energy crisis these exact projects are creating.
Meanwhile, other South Carolina counties are waking up, Spartanburg, Greenwood, Newberry, Chesterfield, and Colleton are advancing or have passed moratoriums on new data centers because of exactly these issues, higher bills, water strain, noise, air quality from backup generation, and traffic. 
A statewide bill (H. 5286) was floated for a pause until 2028. 
🐷Power constraints have already killed or delayed projects elsewhere (e.g., Stream Data Centers in Marion County).
Even though META’s Aiken project is far along with sunk costs and strong local political cover, it’s not immune. 
Growing statewide backlash, potential delays in power delivery or related permits, and resident pushback on expansions or future incentives could still bite. The “LEED Gold” admin building with mass timber is a PR fig leaf on a concrete-and-steel energy beast.
Call it progress if you want. The rest of us see a billionaire-backed land and resource grab dressed up as community salvation. 
Aiken County sold itself cheap. Residents, demand full transparency on power and water deals, fight any more handouts, and make sure future projects pay their real costs. 
Stop rolling out the red carpet for Big Tech’s AI empire at the EXPENSE of the residents.
